├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ steiner_shared_broadcast_tree                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The original algorithm sends every partition of data independently along the shortest     │
│                          │ path to each destination, paying full price for shared links multiple times (once per     │
│                          │ destination, once per partition). The key improvement is to recognize that transfers over │
│                          │ links already used by the broadcast can piggyback at no additional link setup — i.e.,     │
│                          │ build a shared broadcast (Steiner-like) tree instead of independent shortest paths.       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I implement the classic Takahashi–Matsuyama Steiner tree heuristic: start with the        │
│                          │ source, repeatedly connect the nearest unconnected destination to the current tree using  │
│                          │ Dijkstra, but treat edges already in the tree as cost ~0 (they only need to be "paid"     │
│                          │ once, then all traffic riding them reuses the established transfer). This drastically     │
│                          │ reduces redundant transfers across destinations that share network paths (e.g.,           │
│                          │ inter-cloud backbone links), which is exactly the "reduce redundant transfers / balance   │
│                          │ load across networks" objective. Every partition then rides the same shared tree, so the  │
│                          │ total edge cost is paid essentially once per shared link rather than multiplied per       │
│                          │ destination/partition. The output format (per-dst, per-partition edge lists) is preserved │
│                          │ exactly, so downstream evaluation still works.                                            │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
