╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multicast_tree_broadcast                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The current algorithm independently computes a shortest path from src to every            │
│                          │ destination and then duplicates the transfer of every partition along every edge of every │
│                          │ path. This causes massive redundant transfers: if two destinations share a common prefix  │
│                          │ of the route, each partition is transferred multiple times over the shared links. A       │
│                          │ better strategy is to treat the broadcast as a multicast problem: for each partition,     │
│                          │ build a single directed tree rooted at src that spans all destinations (a Steiner-tree    │
│                          │ approximation). Data flows once per edge of the tree, and destinations that share         │
│                          │ upstream links receive data via shared transfer, cutting cost multiplicatively. We use a  │
│                          │ greedy shortest-path-from-tree heuristic (repeatedly attach the closest un-reached        │
│                          │ destination to the growing tree via Dijkstra), which is a classic 2-approximation for     │
│                          │ Steiner trees and dramatically reduces the number of (expensive) inter-cloud edges used.  │
│                          │ We then record each edge once per partition into the BroadCastTopology, and reconstruct   │
│                          │ per-destination partition paths by walking the tree so the output format stays            │
│                          │ compatible. This directly reduces total_cost (the dominant component of the combined      │
│                          │ score) while keeping the same success guarantees since all edges come from the same       │
│                          │ feasible graph.                                                                           │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
