│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ steiner_multiflow_broadcast                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of computing independent shortest paths per destination (which causes redundant   │
│                          │ transfers and load imbalance), this approach models broadcast as a directed               │
│                          │ Steiner-tree-like problem solved via a shared-path heuristic: it computes multiple        │
│                          │ cost-diverse candidate paths per destination (using k-shortest paths with randomized      │
│                          │ edge-weight perturbation to find parallel routes across different networks), then         │
│                          │ greedily builds a shared broadcast tree where a common edge is only paid for once         │
│                          │ conceptually (paths reusing popular edges get higher effective savings). Then it assigns  │
│                          │ partitions to paths with load balancing across edge throughput capacities using a         │
│                          │ min-cost flow-like greedy: each partition is routed along the cheapest candidate path     │
│                          │ that still has residual throughput capacity, spreading traffic across parallel networks.  │
│                          │ This reduces redundant transfers, balances load, and exploits multi-network topologies,   │
│                          │ lowering total cost compared to independent Dijkstra shortest paths.                      │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
