│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ steiner_mst_shared_tree                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach sends every destination along its own independent shortest path, so  │
│                          │ no edge sharing actually happens even though tree_edges are tracked. To reduce total      │
│                          │ transfer cost, replace this with a classic Kou–Markowsky–Berman-Ramaiyer Steiner-tree     │
│                          │ heuristic: (1) run Dijkstra once from src on the sanitized graph `h`, (2) build a metric  │
│                          │ closure over {src} ∪ dsts using pairwise shortest path costs (computed via all-pairs      │
│                          │ Dijkstra restricted to terminals), (3) compute an MST on that closure, (4) union the      │
│                          │ corresponding src→dst shortest paths to form a shared directed tree, and (5) use the      │
│                          │ unique src→dst path inside that tree for each destination when appending partition paths  │
│                          │ via `append_dst_partition_path`. This prunes expensive redundant edges: multiple          │
│                          │ destinations routed through a common hub now reuse the same upstream path, lowering       │
│                          │ overall $/GB while keeping the exact validated I/O contract (whole-edge appends, edge     │
│                          │ data read from `h`, all partitions still receive a path).                                 │
