│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ steiner_shared_tree_broadcast                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current algorithm computes a fully independent shortest path for each destination, so │
│                          │ any link common to multiple destinations is paid for repeatedly (once per destination,    │
│                          │ times each partition). A broadcast is fundamentally a multicast problem: data that        │
│                          │ reaches an intermediate node once can be fanned out to many destinations, so shared edges │
│                          │ should only be paid once. We evolve the search into a greedy Steiner-tree heuristic:      │
│                          │ process destinations sequentially and run Dijkstra with cost discounts on edges already   │
│                          │ used by the growing broadcast tree. This makes later destinations strongly prefer reusing │
│                          │ the existing broadcast backbone instead of paying for redundant parallel transfers. This  │
│                          │ directly reduces the number of distinct paid edges, which is the dominant term in total   │
│                          │ transfer cost. We also add a reachability pre-check so unreachable destinations are       │
│                          │ recorded rather than silently dropped, and use a deterministic RNG seed for               │
│                          │ reproducibility of the perturbed candidate search.                                        │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
