│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ greedy_steiner_perturb_crossover                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ Crossover of the baseline per-destination Dijkstra approach (Program 1: robust, simple,   │
│                          │ guaranteed success) and the current greedy Steiner-tree approach (lowest cost, 756.69).   │
│                          │ The new program keeps the shared-backbone strategy that made the current program          │
│                          │ cheapest: destinations are processed greedily, and any edge already in the multicast tree │
│                          │ is heavily discounted so subsequent destinations reuse existing transfers instead of      │
│                          │ paying for redundant parallel links. It retains the cost backfilling (None -> large       │
│                          │ finite penalty) and explicit reachability checks from the current program for robustness, │
│                          │ plus the random perturbation restarts that occasionally find cheaper tree hook-ins. The   │
│                          │ deterministic Steiner-style tree from Program 2 is blended in by first ordering           │
│                          │ destinations by their plain Dijkstra cost (cheapest first), which grows the backbone      │
│                          │ toward nearby nodes before attaching far ones — empirically improving shared-edge reuse.  │
│                          │ Output format (BroadCastTopology with per-dst per-partition edge lists) is unchanged.     │
